var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"runtime/#Raw-MIL-and-the-runtime","page":"Raw MIL and the runtime","text":"At the lowest level, compile takes MIL program text, the shapes of its input and output buffers, and an optional weight file:\n\nusing AppleNeuralEngine\n\nprogram = compile(mil_source;\n    input_shapes=[(64, 256)], output_shapes=[(64, 256)],\n    input_types=Float32, output_types=Float32,\n    weight_blob=build_weight_blob(W))\n\noutput, = evaluate!(program, input)\nclose!(program)\n\nexamples/minimal.jl is a complete program in this style.","title":"Raw MIL and the runtime"},{"category":"section","location":"runtime/#Buffers","page":"Raw MIL and the runtime","text":"Each input and output is a dense Julia array whose memory matches the MIL tensor. For the usual [1, C, 1, S] activation layout that is an (S, C) matrix. The first Julia dimension is the innermost MIL dimension. evaluate! copies inputs into IOSurfaces shared with the Neural Engine, runs the program, and copies the outputs back.","title":"Buffers"},{"category":"section","location":"runtime/#Weights","page":"Raw MIL and the runtime","text":"All constants live in one file, @model_path/weights/weight.bin, in Core ML's weight-file format. A MIL constant refers to a tensor by its offset:\n\ntensor<fp16, [64, 64, 1, 1]> W = const()[name = string(\"W\"),\n    val = tensor<fp16, [64, 64, 1, 1]>(BLOBFILE(path = string(\"@model_path/weights/weight.bin\"), offset = uint64(64)))];\n\nbuild_weight_blob makes a file holding one fp16 matrix (offset 64).\npack_weights packs several matrices and returns their offsets.\nWeightFile appends arrays of any rank and element type one at a time.\n\nThe runtime can also take weights split across several files, but whether it accepts them then depends on the order of an internal dictionary. This package always uses one file.","title":"Weights"},{"category":"section","location":"runtime/#Input-and-output-order","page":"Raw MIL and the runtime","text":"The runtime binds a program's inputs and outputs to buffers in sorted name order, not in the order they are declared. For func main(b, a) -> (q, p) the first buffer is a and the first output is p. The graph builder names inputs and outputs so that both orders agree.","title":"Input and output order"},{"category":"section","location":"runtime/#Errors","page":"Raw MIL and the runtime","text":"Compilation and evaluation failures raise an ANEError carrying the runtime's own message, for example InvalidMILProgram when the ANE compiler's MIL validation rejects the program.","title":"Errors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Compiling-and-running","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Compiling and running"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Weights","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Weights"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#MIL-graph-builder","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"MIL graph builder"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Ops","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Ops"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Tracing","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Tracing"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.compile","page":"API reference","text":"compile(mil; input_shapes, output_shapes, weight_blob=nothing,\n        input_types=Float32, output_types=Float32)\n\nCompile one MIL program with the private ANE runtime and load it onto the Neural Engine. Inputs and outputs are contiguous buffers of Float32 or Float16 (one type for all, or one per tensor, matching the MIL program); each shape gives the size of the Julia array that is copied in or out, and its memory layout must match the tensor in the MIL program.\n\nweight_blob is the program's weight file, @model_path/weights/weight.bin, usually made with build_weight_blob or, for several tensors, pack_weights.\n\nThe runtime binds MIL inputs and outputs to buffer indices in sorted name order, not declaration order.\n\n\n\n\n\ncompile(g::MIL.Graph) -> ANEProgram\n\nCompile a program built with the MIL graph builder. Its inputs and outputs are Julia arrays with the MIL dimensions reversed and singleton dimensions dropped: a MIL [1, C, 1, S] tensor is a Julia (S, C) matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\ncompile(f, inputs...) -> ANEProgram\n\nTrace f (see trace) and compile it for the Neural Engine. The program is callable: program(x...) returns the output (or a tuple of outputs).\n\nW = randn(Float32, 512, 2048)\nprogram = compile(x -> max.(x * W, 0), randn(Float16, 256, 512))\ny = program(x)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.compile"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.trace","page":"API reference","text":"trace(f, inputs...) -> MIL.Graph\n\nRecord f applied to placeholders for inputs (example arrays, or sizes (n,) / (m, n), which mean Float16 inputs) as a MIL graph. f returns an array or a tuple of arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.trace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.ANEProgram","page":"API reference","text":"ANEProgram\n\nA MIL program compiled and loaded onto the Neural Engine, with its input and output buffers. Create one with compile; run it with evaluate! or by calling it, program(inputs...). A program holds Neural Engine resources until close! is called or it is garbage collected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.ANEProgram"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.evaluate!","page":"API reference","text":"evaluate!(program, inputs...) -> Vector{Array}\n\nCopy inputs into the program's input buffers, run it on the Neural Engine and return freshly allocated output arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.evaluate!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.close!","page":"API reference","text":"close!(program::ANEProgram)\n\nUnload program from the Neural Engine and free its buffers. Safe to call more than once; afterwards the program cannot be evaluated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.close!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Base.isopen","page":"API reference","text":"isopen(program::ANEProgram) -> Bool\n\nWhether program is still loaded (not yet close!d).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.isopen"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.initialize!","page":"API reference","text":"initialize!()\n\nLoad the private AppleNeuralEngine framework and check that the classes this package relies on exist. Called automatically by compile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.initialize!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.functional","page":"API reference","text":"functional() -> Bool\n\nWhether programs can run on a Neural Engine here: Apple Silicon, the private framework present, and a physical (not virtualized) ANE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.functional"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.device_info","page":"API reference","text":"device_info() -> NamedTuple\n\nProperties of this machine's Neural Engine, as reported by the ANE framework: has_ane, virtual_machine, num_ane, num_cores, architecture (e.g. \"h14g\") and subtype.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.device_info"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.ANEError","page":"API reference","text":"ANEError(msg)\n\nRaised when the ANE runtime rejects a model or fails to run it. msg includes the runtime's own NSError description when one is available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.ANEError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.WeightFile","page":"API reference","text":"WeightFile()\n\nAn ANE weight file that tensors are appended to one at a time with push!(file, array), which returns the offset a MIL BLOBFILE uses to refer to the tensor. bytes(file) returns the file contents for compile.\n\nArrays are stored in MIL (row-major) order: element A[i, j, k] is MIL element [i, j, k], so a conv weight [Cout, Cin, kh, kw] is the Julia array W[cout, cin, kh, kw]. The element type is kept; supported types are Float16, Float32, Int16, Int32, Int8, UInt16, UInt8.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.WeightFile"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.pack_weights","page":"API reference","text":"pack_weights(tensors...; transposed=false) -> (blob, offsets)\n\nEncode several Float32 matrices as fp16 tensors in one ANE weight file. Pass blob to compile as weight_blob and refer to tensor i in the MIL program as\n\nBLOBFILE(path = string(\"@model_path/weights/weight.bin\"), offset = uint64(offsets[i]))\n\nEach tensor is stored in row-major order, so a conv weight [Cout, Cin, 1, 1] is the matrix W[cout, cin]; with transposed=true every tensor is stored transposed. A vector (e.g. a per-channel scale) can be passed as a 1×n matrix. See WeightFile for tensors of other ranks and element types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.pack_weights"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.build_weight_blob","page":"API reference","text":"build_weight_blob(weights::AbstractMatrix{Float32}; transposed=false)\n\nEncode one Float32 matrix as an fp16 ANE weight file, referred to in MIL as BLOBFILE(path = string(\"@model_path/weights/weight.bin\"), offset = uint64(64)). See pack_weights for several tensors and for the storage order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.build_weight_blob"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.bytes","page":"API reference","text":"bytes(file::WeightFile) -> Vector{UInt8}\n\nThe contents of the weight file, to pass to compile as weight_blob.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.bytes"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL","page":"API reference","text":"MIL\n\nA builder for MIL programs, the input language of the ANE compiler. Ops are Julia functions on Vars that infer output types and shapes, so the generated program is always well-typed:\n\nusing AppleNeuralEngine, AppleNeuralEngine.MIL\ng = MIL.Graph()\nx = MIL.input(g, :x, Float16, (1, 64, 1, 32))\nW = MIL.constant(g, randn(Float32, 64, 64, 1, 1))\nMIL.output!(g, MIL.relu(MIL.conv(x, W)))\nprogram = compile(g)\n\nFunction names and arguments follow the MIL op set as documented by coremltools; several shadow Base names (exp, reshape, ...), so call them qualified (MIL.exp) rather than using the module.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Graph","page":"API reference","text":"Graph(; opset=\"ios18\")\n\nA MIL program being built. Create inputs with input, apply ops to the resulting Vars, mark results with output!, then pass the graph to compile or print it with source.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Graph"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var","page":"API reference","text":"Var\n\nA value in a Graph: a tensor with a MIL element type and a MIL shape (row-major, outermost dimension first; () for a scalar). Vars are produced by input, constant and the op functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.input","page":"API reference","text":"input(g, name, T, shape; julia_shape=nothing) -> Var\n\nDeclare a program input of element type T (Float32 or Float16) and MIL shape shape. Inputs are passed to evaluate! in declaration order, as Julia arrays of size julia_shape; by default that is shape reversed with singleton dimensions dropped, the array with the same memory layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.input"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.output!","page":"API reference","text":"output!(g, v, name=v.name; julia_shape=nothing)\n\nMark v as a program output. Outputs are returned by evaluate! in the order they are marked, as Julia arrays of size julia_shape (see input). They must be Float32 or Float16.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.output!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.constant","page":"API reference","text":"constant(g, value; eltype=Float16) -> Var\n\nA constant tensor. Arrays are stored in the program's weight file, converted to eltype and indexed the MIL way (A[i, j] is MIL element [i, j]); see WeightFile. Numbers become scalar constants.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.constant"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.constant_int8","page":"API reference","text":"constant_int8(g, W; axis=0) -> Var\n\nA constant stored as int8 with one fp16 scale per slice along axis (symmetric quantization), dequantized by the ANE when the program is loaded (constexpr_affine_dequantize). Halves the weight memory of fp16 constants.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.constant_int8"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.op!","page":"API reference","text":"op!(g, op, args, outputs; name=op) -> Var or Tuple{Var...}\n\nAppend the MIL operation op to g. args are name => value pairs (values are Vars or literals; nothing omits an optional argument) and outputs are (eltype, shape) pairs, one per result. This is the escape hatch for ops that have no wrapper; the wrappers in this module call it after inferring shapes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.op!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.source","page":"API reference","text":"source(g) -> String\n\nThe MIL text of g, as passed to the ANE compiler.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.source"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.weight_blob","page":"API reference","text":"weight_blob(g) -> Union{Vector{UInt8},Nothing}\n\nThe weight file holding g's constants, or nothing if it has none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.weight_blob"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.cast-Tuple{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var, DataType}","page":"API reference","text":"cast(x, T)\n\nConvert x to element type T (Float16, Float32, Int32, Int16, UInt8, Bool, ...).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.cast"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.constant_int8-Tuple{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Graph, AbstractArray{<:Real}}","page":"API reference","text":"constant_int8(g, W; axis=0) -> Var\n\nA constant stored as int8 with one fp16 scale per slice along axis (symmetric quantization), dequantized by the ANE when the program is loaded (constexpr_affine_dequantize). Halves the weight memory of fp16 constants.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.constant_int8"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.conv-Tuple{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var, AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var}","page":"API reference","text":"conv(x, weight; bias=nothing, strides=1, pad_type=\"valid\", pad=nothing, dilations=1, groups=1)\n\nx is [N, Cin, spatial...], weight is [Cout, Cin ÷ groups, kernel...]. pad_type is \"valid\", \"same\", \"same_lower\" or \"custom\" (then pad gives [begin, end] per spatial dimension).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.conv"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.conv_transpose-Tuple{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var, AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var}","page":"API reference","text":"conv_transpose(x, weight; bias=nothing, strides=1, pad_type=\"valid\", pad=nothing, dilations=1, groups=1)\n\nweight is [Cin, Cout ÷ groups, kernel...].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.conv_transpose"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.einsum-Tuple{AbstractString, Vararg{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var}}","page":"API reference","text":"einsum(equation, values...)\n\nMIL supports a fixed set of equations, e.g. \"nchw,nwhu->nchu\" (batched matmul over the last two dimensions), for two rank-3 or rank-4 inputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.einsum"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.gelu-Tuple{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var}","page":"API reference","text":"gelu(x; mode=\"EXACT\")\n\nmode is \"EXACT\", \"TANH_APPROXIMATION\" or \"SIGMOID_APPROXIMATION\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.gelu"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.layer_norm-Tuple{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var}","page":"API reference","text":"layer_norm(x; axes=[-1], gamma=nothing, beta=nothing, epsilon=1e-5)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.layer_norm"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.linear-Tuple{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var, AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var}","page":"API reference","text":"linear(x, weight; bias=nothing)\n\nx * weight' .+ bias over the last dimension; weight is [out, in].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.linear"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.lstm-NTuple{5, AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var}","page":"API reference","text":"lstm(x, initial_h, initial_c, weight_ih, weight_hh; bias=nothing, output_sequence=false, ...) -> (output, h, c)\n\nx is [T, B, I]; the weights are [4H, I] and [4H, H] (gate order i, f, o, g).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.lstm"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.matmul-Tuple{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var, AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var}","page":"API reference","text":"matmul(x, y; transpose_x=false, transpose_y=false)\n\nBatched matrix product over the last two dimensions, with broadcasting over the rest.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.matmul"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.pad-Tuple{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"pad(x, pad; mode=\"constant\", constant_val=0)\n\npad lists [begin, end] amounts for the last length(pad) ÷ 2 dimensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.pad"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.reshape-Tuple{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"reshape(x, shape)\n\nshape may contain one -1 (inferred) and 0 (copy the input dimension).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.reshape"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.scaled_dot_product_attention-Tuple{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var, AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var, AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var}","page":"API reference","text":"scaled_dot_product_attention(query, key, value; attn_mask=nothing)\n\nsoftmax(query * key' / sqrt(E) + attn_mask) * value over the last two dimensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.scaled_dot_product_attention"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.select-Tuple{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var, Union{Number, AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var}, Union{Number, AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var}}","page":"API reference","text":"select(cond, a, b)\n\nElementwise cond ? a : b with broadcasting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.select"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.slice_by_index-Tuple{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"slice_by_index(x, begin_, end_; stride=nothing, begin_mask=nothing, end_mask=nothing, squeeze_mask=nothing)\n\nPython-style half-open slices begin_:stride:end_ per dimension (0-based).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.slice_by_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.softmax-Tuple{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var}","page":"API reference","text":"softmax(x; axis=-1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.softmax"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.split-Tuple{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var}","page":"API reference","text":"split(x; axis, num_splits=nothing, split_sizes=nothing) -> Tuple{Var...}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.split"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.topk-Tuple{AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.Var}","page":"API reference","text":"topk(x; k=1, axis=-1, ascending=false) -> (values, indices)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.MIL.topk"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AppleNeuralEngine.Traced","page":"API reference","text":"Traced\n\nA placeholder for a Julia vector or matrix while a function is traced by compile(f, inputs...). Supports broadcasting with arbitrary scalar functions built from arithmetic, exp, log, sqrt, tanh, abs, max, min, comparisons and ifelse; matrix products and adjoint; and sum, maximum, minimum with dims.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.Traced"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Op-support","page":"Op support","text":"Generated by tools/probe_ops.jl on Apple M2 Max, macOS 27.0.1, 2026-10-29. Each op runs in a small fp16 program; err is the maximum absolute error relative to the largest reference value (fp16 rounding gives ~1e-3); ≈ marks results within 10% (a coarse hardware approximation). \"compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\" means the ANE compiler's MIL validation rejected the op; every argument encoding we tried (inline or const literals, other axes, other modes) was rejected. Core ML may lower these ops to others before they reach the ANE. The ANE is a private, undocumented target, so this table can change with macOS releases. Re-run the probe on your machine.\n\n129 of 167 probed cases compile and run.\n\nNot probed (they need control flow, dynamic shapes or state, which this package does not support yet): cond, while_loop, make_list and the list_* ops, non_zero, non_maximum_suppression, classify, read_state, slice_update, affine, resample, crop_resize, reshape_like, and the constexpr_* compression ops other than constexpr_affine_dequantize.\n\nObserved constraints, independent of the op:\n\nComputation must be fp16: the same ops on fp32 tensors do not compile (cast inputs).\nA trailing identity op cannot be a program output.\nTensor buffers need a 64-byte aligned row stride, which this package provides, so any innermost size works.","title":"Op support"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Elementwise-unary","page":"Op support","text":"op status err note\nabs ✓ 0.0 \nacos ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nasin ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\natan ≈ 0.025 approximate\natanh ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nceil ✓ 0.0 \ncos ✗  compile fails (CompilationFailure)\ncosh ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nerf ✓ 0.00084 \nexp ✓ 0.00076 \nexp2 ✓ 0.00036 \nfloor ✓ 0.0 \nround ✓ 0.0 \nsign ✓ 0.0 \nsin ✗  compile fails (CompilationFailure)\nsinh ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nsqrt ✓ 0.00077 \nsquare ✓ 0.00029 \ntan ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\ntanh ✓ 0.0016 \ninverse ✓ 0.00058 \nlog ✓ 0.001 \nrsqrt ✓ 0.0014 \nrsqrt (no epsilon) ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nthreshold ✓ 1.4e-5 \nclip ✓ 0.0 \ncast (fp16→fp32→fp16) ✗  compile fails (CompilationFailure)\nlogical_not ✓ 0.0 ","title":"Elementwise unary"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Activation","page":"Op support","text":"op status err note\nrelu ✓ 0.0 \nrelu6 ✓ 0.0 \nsigmoid ✓ 0.0034 \nsilu ✓ 0.0043 \nsoftplus ✓ 0.00076 \nsoftsign ✓ 0.00092 \ngelu ✓ 0.0016 \ngelu (tanh approx.) ✓ 0.0018 \nelu ✓ 0.00094 \nleaky_relu ✓ 5.5e-5 \nthresholded_relu ✓ 0.0 \nclamped_relu ✓ 0.00015 \nlinear_activation ✓ 0.00025 \nscaled_tanh ✓ 0.0017 \nsigmoid_hard ✓ 0.00029 \nprelu ✓ 9.4e-5 \nsoftplus_parametric ✓ 0.0012 \nsoftmax ✓ 0.00074 ","title":"Activation"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Elementwise-binary","page":"Op support","text":"op status err note\nadd ✓ 0.00036 \nsub ✓ 0.00033 \nmul ✓ 0.00028 \nreal_div ✓ 0.00073 \npow ✓ 0.00075 \nmaximum ✓ 0.0 \nminimum ✓ 0.0 \nfloor_div ≈ 0.5 off by one when the quotient is (nearly) an integer: computed with an approximate reciprocal\nmod ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nadd (broadcast [1,C,1,1]) ✓ 0.00036 \nmul (scalar literal) ✓ 0.00037 \nequal ✓ 0.0 \nnot_equal ✓ 0.0 \ngreater ✓ 0.0 \ngreater_equal ✓ 0.0 \nless ✓ 0.0 \nless_equal ✓ 0.0 \nlogical_and ✓ 0.0 \nlogical_or ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nlogical_xor ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nselect ✓ 0.0 ","title":"Elementwise binary"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Reduction","page":"Op support","text":"op status err note\nreduce_sum axes=[1] ✓ 0.001 \nreduce_sum axes=[3] ✓ 0.00065 \nreduce_sum axes=[2, 3] ✓ 0.0011 \nreduce_mean axes=[1] ✓ 0.00057 \nreduce_mean axes=[3] ✓ 0.00074 \nreduce_mean axes=[2, 3] ✓ 0.00066 \nreduce_max axes=[1] ✓ 0.0 \nreduce_max axes=[3] ✓ 0.0 \nreduce_max axes=[2, 3] ✓ 0.0 \nreduce_min axes=[1] ✓ 0.0 \nreduce_min axes=[3] ✓ 0.0 \nreduce_min axes=[2, 3] ✓ 0.0 \nreduce_prod axes=[1] ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nreduce_l1_norm axes=[1] ✓ 0.0012 \nreduce_l1_norm axes=[3] ✓ 0.00085 \nreduce_l1_norm axes=[2, 3] ✓ 0.001 \nreduce_l2_norm axes=[1] ✓ 0.00077 \nreduce_l2_norm axes=[3] ✓ 0.00055 \nreduce_l2_norm axes=[2, 3] ✓ 0.00072 \nreduce_sum_square axes=[1] ✓ 0.00063 \nreduce_sum_square axes=[3] ✓ 0.00072 \nreduce_sum_square axes=[2, 3] ✓ 0.00096 \nreduce_log_sum axes=[1] ✓ 0.0014 \nreduce_log_sum axes=[3] ✓ 0.0011 \nreduce_log_sum axes=[2, 3] ✓ 0.0012 \nreduce_log_sum_exp axes=[1] ✓ 0.00098 \nreduce_log_sum_exp axes=[3] ✓ 0.001 \nreduce_log_sum_exp axes=[2, 3] ✓ 0.001 \nreduce_argmax ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nreduce_argmin ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)","title":"Reduction"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Convolution","page":"Op support","text":"op status err note\nconv 1x1 ✓ 0.00036 \nconv 3x3 same ✓ 0.0004 \nconv 3x3 stride 2 ✓ 0.00034 \nconv + bias ✓ 0.00051 \nconv depthwise 3x3 ✓ 0.00034 \nconv int8 weights (constexpr_affine_dequantize) ✓ 0.0044 \nconv_transpose 2x2 stride 2 ✓ runs  no reference\nconv 1D ✓ runs  no reference","title":"Convolution"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Pooling","page":"Op support","text":"op status err note\nmax_pool ✓ 0.0 \navg_pool ✓ 0.00068 \nl2_pool ✓ 0.00051 ","title":"Pooling"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Linear-algebra","page":"Op support","text":"op status err note\nmatmul ✓ 0.00045 \nmatmul transpose_y ✓ 0.00028 \nmatmul (constant) ✓ 0.00046 \nlinear ✓ 0.00045 \neinsum nchw,nwhu->nchu ✓ 0.00032 ","title":"Linear algebra"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Normalization","page":"Op support","text":"op status err note\nlayer_norm ✓ 0.00091 \nlayer_norm gamma/beta (last axis) ✓ 0.001 \nbatch_norm ✓ 0.00056 \ninstance_norm ✓ 0.0012 \nl2_norm ✓ runs  no reference\nlocal_response_norm ✓ runs  no reference","title":"Normalization"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Tensor-transformation","page":"Op support","text":"op status err note\nreshape ✓ 0.0 \ntranspose ✓ 0.0 \ntranspose (last two) ✓ 0.0 \nexpand_dims + squeeze ✓ 0.0 \nreverse ✓ 2.2e-6 \nslice_by_index ✓ 0.0 \nslice_by_index (stride 2) ✓ 0.0 \nslice_by_size ✓ 0.0 \ndepth_to_space ✓ runs  no reference\nspace_to_depth ✓ runs  no reference\npixel_shuffle ✓ runs  no reference\npixel_unshuffle ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nsliding_windows ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)","title":"Tensor transformation"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Tensor-operation","page":"Op support","text":"op status err note\nconcat ✓ 0.0 \nconcat (last axis) ✓ 0.0 \nsplit ✓ 0.0 \nstack ✓ 0.0 \ntile ✓ 0.0 \npad (constant) ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\npad (reflect) ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nflatten2d ✓ 0.0 \nband_part ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\ncumsum ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\ntopk ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nargsort ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\none_hot ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nfill ✓ 0.00037 \nrange_1d ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nshape ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nidentity ✓ 0.0 ","title":"Tensor operation"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Scatter/gather","page":"Op support","text":"op status err note\ngather ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\ngather_along_axis ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\ngather_nd ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nscatter ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nscatter_along_axis ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nscatter_nd ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)","title":"Scatter/gather"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Image-resizing","page":"Op support","text":"op status err note\nupsample_nearest_neighbor ✓ 2.2e-6 \nupsample_bilinear ✓ runs  no reference\nresize_nearest_neighbor ✓ 1.9e-6 \nresize_bilinear ✓ runs  no reference\ncrop ✓ 0.00013 ","title":"Image resizing"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Attention","page":"Op support","text":"op status err note\nscaled_dot_product_attention ✓ 0.0014 ","title":"Attention"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Recurrent","page":"Op support","text":"op status err note\nlstm ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\ngru ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nrnn ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)","title":"Recurrent"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Quantization","page":"Op support","text":"op status err note\nquantize → dequantize ✓ 0.014 ","title":"Quantization"},{"category":"section","location":"op_support/#Random","page":"Op support","text":"op status err note\nrandom_normal ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)\nrandom_uniform ✗  compile fails (InvalidMILProgram)","title":"Random"},{"category":"section","location":"builder/#The-MIL-graph-builder","page":"The MIL graph builder","text":"MIL (\"Model Intermediate Language\") is the program format the ANE compiler reads, the same one Core ML uses. AppleNeuralEngine.MIL builds MIL programs from Julia. Every op is a function on MIL.Vars that infers its output type and shape, so the generated program is always well-typed. Names, constants, weight files and input/output ordering are handled for you.\n\nusing AppleNeuralEngine\nconst M = AppleNeuralEngine.MIL\n\ng = M.Graph()\nx = M.input(g, :x, Float16, (1, 64, 1, 32))        # MIL shape [N, C, H, W]\nW = M.constant(g, randn(Float32, 128, 64, 1, 1))    # stored as fp16 weights\nh = M.relu(M.conv(x, W))\nM.output!(g, M.reduce_mean(h; axes=[1], keep_dims=true), :y)\n\nprogram = compile(g)\ny = program(randn(Float16, 32, 64))                 # Julia sizes: see below\nprint(M.source(g))                                  # the generated MIL text","title":"The MIL graph builder"},{"category":"section","location":"builder/#Conventions","page":"The MIL graph builder","text":"Shapes and axes are MIL's: row-major, outermost dimension first, 0-based axes, negative axes count from the end. Op and argument names follow the coremltools MIL op reference. Several names shadow Base (exp, reshape, split, ...), so call them qualified: M.exp(x).\nConstants are indexed the MIL way: A[i, j, k] is MIL element [i, j, k]. A conv weight [Cout, Cin, kh, kw] is the Julia array W[cout, cin, kh, kw]. M.constant_int8 stores int8 weights with per-channel scales.\nProgram inputs and outputs are Julia arrays with the same memory as the MIL tensor: the dimensions reversed and singleton dimensions dropped. A MIL [1, C, 1, S] tensor is a Julia (S, C) matrix. Pass julia_shape= to MIL.input / MIL.output! to choose another shape with the same number of elements.\nCompute in fp16. Inputs and outputs may be Float32 or Float16, but ops on fp32 tensors do not compile: M.cast inputs to Float16 first. Float16 inputs and outputs also halve the data copied per call.","title":"Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"builder/#Escape-hatch","page":"The MIL graph builder","text":"Ops without a wrapper can be emitted with MIL.op!, giving the output types yourself:\n\ny = M.op!(g, \"softsign\", (:x => x,), ((Float16, size(x)),))","title":"Escape hatch"},{"category":"section","location":"builder/#Building-on-a-trace","page":"The MIL graph builder","text":"trace returns the MIL.Graph of a Julia function, so a traced model can be inspected (M.source) or extended before compile.","title":"Building on a trace"},{"category":"section","location":"hardware/#Hardware-notes","page":"Hardware notes","text":"Observations made while building and testing this package on an M2 Max (16-core ANE, macOS 27). They complement the restrictions catalogued by Orion (arXiv 2603.06728).","title":"Hardware notes"},{"category":"section","location":"hardware/#Precision","page":"Hardware notes","text":"The ANE computes in fp16. Ops on fp32 tensors do not compile, and neither does a cast to fp32 in the middle of a graph. Only program inputs and outputs may be fp32.\nround rounds ties away from zero, computed as sign(x)·floor(|x| + 0.5) in fp16. For |x| just below a half-integer the addition can round up.\nfloor_div uses an approximate reciprocal and can be off by one when the quotient is (nearly) an integer. atan is accurate to about 2.5%.","title":"Precision"},{"category":"section","location":"hardware/#Buffers","page":"Hardware notes","text":"Row alignment. Tensor buffers are 2-D IOSurfaces with one row per run of the innermost MIL dimension. The ANE rejects rows that are not 64-byte aligned (16 fp32 or 32 fp16 values). The runtime reports this as an evaluation failure. With the row stride padded to 64 bytes, any innermost size works, down to\nThis package does that, so the \"pad the sequence dimension to ≥ 16\" rule\nreported elsewhere does not apply.\nBuffer sizes must match what the compiled model expects. The runtime reports \"IOSurface smaller than the model expects\" otherwise.","title":"Buffers"},{"category":"section","location":"hardware/#Program-structure","page":"Hardware notes","text":"Inputs and outputs are bound in sorted-name order (see Raw MIL and the runtime).\nA program output cannot be produced by a trailing identity op.\nVariable names must not be MIL keywords (const, tensor, type names).","title":"Program structure"},{"category":"section","location":"hardware/#Cost","page":"Hardware notes","text":"Compiling and loading a program takes about 20 ms for a tiny graph and about 70–80 ms for a transformer block. We ran 300 compile/run/close cycles in one process without hitting a compile limit, as long as programs are closed.\nEach evaluation costs about 0.1–0.2 ms on top of the compute, for the call and the buffer copies. Small programs are therefore latency-bound. Batch work into larger matrix-matrix products.\nCalling the runtime through ObjectiveC.jl instead of a compiled Objective-C bridge adds about 0.1 µs per call, which is not measurable against the ANE itself.","title":"Cost"},{"category":"section","location":"tracing/#Tracing-Julia-functions","page":"Tracing Julia functions","text":"compile(f, inputs...) runs f once on placeholder arrays, records every operation as a MIL graph, and compiles the graph for the Neural Engine. The same function keeps working on ordinary arrays, so you can check the ANE result against the CPU directly.\n\nusing AppleNeuralEngine\n\nconst EPS = 1f-5\nsilu(z) = z / (1 + exp(-z))\n\n# A LLaMA-style feed-forward block: RMSNorm, SwiGLU, residual.\nfunction ffn(x)\n    n = x ./ sqrt.(sum(abs2, x; dims=2) ./ size(x, 2) .+ EPS) .* g'\n    return x .+ (silu.(n * W1') .* (n * W3')) * W2'\nend\n\nx = randn(Float16, 256, 512)\nprogram = compile(ffn, x)        # inputs: example arrays (or sizes)\ny = program(x)                   # on the ANE\ny_cpu = ffn(Float32.(x))         # the same function on the CPU","title":"Tracing Julia functions"},{"category":"section","location":"tracing/#What-is-supported","page":"Tracing Julia functions","text":"Inputs are vectors or matrices of Float16 or Float32. The output has the same element type as the first input. Inside the trace everything is computed in fp16.\n\nBroadcasting with any scalar function built from + - * / ^, exp, exp2, log, sqrt, inv, abs, abs2, tanh, max, min, floor, ceil, round, sign, comparisons and ifelse. Whole expressions such as silu.(x) or ifelse.(x .> 0, x, 0.1f0 .* x) are traced through the scalar function.\nMatrix products A * B between traced arrays and constant matrices, adjoint/transpose, and matrix-vector products.\nReductions sum, maximum, minimum, with or without dims, and sum(f, x; dims).\nConstants: any Julia array captured by the function (weights, biases) is stored in the program's weight file. An array used several times is stored once.\nSeveral outputs: return a tuple.\n\nx * W with a constant W becomes a 1×1 convolution, the Neural Engine's fastest operation, so write layers in that orientation: rows are tokens or samples, columns are features.","title":"What is supported"},{"category":"section","location":"tracing/#Layout","page":"Tracing Julia functions","text":"A traced (T, D) matrix is held as the MIL tensor [1, D, 1, T]. That is the same memory as the Julia matrix and the layout the ANE is fastest with, so inputs and outputs need no transposition.","title":"Layout"},{"category":"section","location":"tracing/#Limitations","page":"Tracing Julia functions","text":"Only vectors and matrices; no indexing, getindex, reshape or mutation.\nsin, cos and tan are rejected by the ANE compiler (see Op support).\nControl flow must not depend on array values. The trace records one path.\nFor anything else, drop to The MIL graph builder: trace returns the graph, which you can extend before compiling.","title":"Limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"#AppleNeuralEngine.jl","page":"Home","text":"Run Julia code on the Apple Neural Engine (ANE), the fp16 neural-network accelerator in every Apple Silicon Mac.\n\nusing AppleNeuralEngine\n\nW = randn(Float32, 512, 2048) ./ 23\nlayer(x) = max.(x * W, 0)                 # ordinary Julia\n\nx = randn(Float16, 256, 512)\nprogram = compile(layer, x)               # traced, compiled for the ANE\ny = program(x)                            # runs on the Neural Engine\n\nThe package drives Apple's private AppleNeuralEngine.framework directly from Julia through ObjectiveC.jl. There is no native code to build and no Core ML model to export. It offers three levels of control:\n\nlevel you write see\ntracing plain Julia on matrices: broadcasting, *, sum, ... Tracing Julia functions\ngraph builder MIL operations with inferred types, e.g. MIL.conv(x, W) The MIL graph builder\nraw runtime MIL program text and buffers Raw MIL and the runtime","title":"AppleNeuralEngine.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"AppleNeuralEngine\")\n\nRequirements: an Apple Silicon Mac and Julia 1.10 or later. Nothing else needs to be installed.\n\nwarning: Private API\nThe ANE has no public programming interface; Apple exposes it only through Core ML. This package uses undocumented framework classes that may change with any macOS release. initialize!() checks that the classes it needs exist. Do not rely on it for production systems.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-the-Neural-Engine-is-good-at","page":"Home","text":"fp16 matrix-matrix work. A transformer feed-forward block (512 → 1536 → 512, 256 tokens) runs in about 0.25 ms on an M2 Max, roughly 5 TFLOP/s. That is about 15× faster than Float32 BLAS on the CPU (examples/transformer_ffn.jl).\nFixed graphs with constant weights. Weights are compiled into the program.\nActivations, normalizations, reductions, convolutions, attention. See Op support.\n\nIt is less suited to small, latency-bound work. Every call costs about 0.1–0.2 ms on top of the compute, and the hardware computes only in fp16 (see Hardware notes).","title":"What the Neural Engine is good at"},{"category":"section","location":"#Acknowledgements","page":"Home","text":"maderix/ANE reverse-engineered the private _ANEInMemoryModel workflow that this package uses: compiling MIL in memory, the model directory layout, and IOSurface-backed requests.\nObjectiveC.jl makes the Objective-C calls possible, and Metal.jl showed how to build an Apple accelerator package on it.\nApple's coremltools documents the MIL op set that the graph builder follows.\nOrion catalogs ANE constraints; our Hardware notes compare against them.\nMuch of the package was written together with Claude, Anthropic's AI assistant.","title":"Acknowledgements"}]
}
